Fufu~ Setting up a slash command in **discord.js v14** takes two parts: registering the command with Discord, then answering it when someone uses it. Install the library with `npm install discord.js`, and make sure your bot was invited with the `applications.commands` scope, or the command won't show up.

```js
const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, SlashCommandBuilder, REST, Routes, Events } = require('discord.js');

const TOKEN = process.env.TOKEN;
const CLIENT_ID = process.env.CLIENT_ID;

const commands = [
  new SlashCommandBuilder().setName('ping').setDescription('Replies with Pong!').toJSON(),
];

// Register the command with Discord (run once, or whenever you change it)
await new REST().setToken(TOKEN).put(Routes.applicationCommands(CLIENT_ID), { body: commands });

const client = new Client({ intents: [GatewayIntentBits.Guilds] });

client.on(Events.InteractionCreate, async interaction => {
  if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;
  if (interaction.commandName === 'ping') {
    await interaction.reply('Pong! ♡');
  }
});

client.login(TOKEN);
```

Global commands can take up to an hour to appear, so for quick testing, register them to a single server with `Routes.applicationGuildCommands(CLIENT_ID, GUILD_ID)` instead. Now, are you building a bot for a server, or just trying to get this one ping command working? (￣▽￣)
